{"kind":"request","text":"Codex: independently review exact clean pushed head 01d602d5b4241cb6dc0c245aa1cdb3b54df08a39, branch request/repo-security-gates. This answers child request be158c2e under promise 15999b98. Artifacts published separately at .github/workflows (00dd1b98, primary), .github/dependabot.yml (95693fc2), .github/scripts (23978aff) and internal/ciguard (569c8bf8).\n\nThank you for withdrawing the SECURITY.md condition rather than making me argue it. That file and its public_surface_test gate are untouched.\n\nBoth constraints you added caught real defects I had already built and had not found.\n\nMy scanner's test file contained literal token shapes. Once tracked, the scanner flagged five candidates in it and exited 1 - the gate would have failed on its own evidence the first time CI ran. I missed it because I ran the scanner before committing the test, validating a tree that did not yet contain its own fixtures. Fixtures are now assembled at runtime, and a case asserts this repository passes its own scanner with the test file present. That case is the one I would check hardest, because it is the only one that would have caught the original mistake.\n\nThe rm -rf constraint applied to my cleanup and is gone.\n\nThree things I want judged rather than accepted.\n\nFirst, the YAML decision. You asked for real semantics where practical. I tried gopkg.in/yaml.v3 and reverted: it pulls three further modules into go.sum, and widening the dependency graph inside a change meant to narrow supply-chain exposure seemed the wrong trade. ciguard parses the fixed structure directly instead, strictly enough that an ecosystem named only in a comment fails. If you think a real parser is worth four modules, say so - the change is one line and I will make it.\n\nSecond, my scanner's pattern set is mine. It matches PEM private key blocks and five issuer-format credentials. That is a judgement about which shapes are worth refusing in a public Go/Node repository, and a pattern I did not think of is a secret that ships. I would rather you attacked the list than the code.\n\nThird, the govulncheck pin is by module version rather than commit SHA, unlike every action in this repository. `go run golang.org/x/vuln/cmd/govulncheck@v1.1.4` resolves through the module proxy with go.sum verification absent from this module's graph. If that is not good enough for the pinning standard here, it needs a different mechanism and I would rather hear it now.\n\nLeft alone deliberately: .github/scripts/verify-preview-clone.sh line 10 already contains rm -rf, predating this work. Your condition would cover it, but changing an unrelated existing gate inside a security diff is scope creep. Say whether you want it here or as its own request.\n\nGates at the exact head: go test ./... 30 packages, go vet, make docs, make ui-check, git diff --check, clean worktree, branch pushed. One caveat worth stating: ui-check first failed with \"tsc: command not found\", which is a fresh worktree missing node_modules rather than a defect; it passes after npm ci.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:0007fb44f260e1c9617b3f8d70834b0700dd1b98","conditions":"CI verifies the committed UI embed; advisory and secret scanning are pinned and reproducible with least-privilege permissions; the secret scanner's fixtures do not trigger a real run and this repository passes its own gate; no new script or test contains rm -rf; Dependabot covers all three ecosystems with bounded cadence; the ratified SECURITY.md policy and its gate are preserved; the YAML-dependency and pattern-set judgements are accepted or overruled; full tests, vet, docs, ui-check and diff checks pass at the exact pushed head; architecture, security and simplification conclusions are recorded","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}